Durarara!! AU
Chapter 26 – Part 1
Curtis walked the street with a bright smile that did not belong to guns, debts, or Yagiri cars.
The flyer was still in his pocket. Long black braids shifted against a blue blazer as he cut through the late afternoon crowd, mismatched eyes already on a workshop door that promised solder, plastic shells, and a free game at the end of the work.
“This is going to be so sick.” The words came out unguarded, almost boyish. “I’m going to build two and put one in his memorial box.”
Justice’s name did not need saying. The second Gameboy already had a destination.
He was about to turn the corner when a girl crashed into his arms.
Smaller than him. White cap. Brown hair. Pink tie. The impact knocked the smile off his face and replaced it with instinct — hands catching her before she hit the pavement.
“Woah— you good? Wait a minute—”
The scarf of her collar had slipped. A scar sat on her neck, pale and wrong against ordinary skin, the exact shape he had priced in Namie’s car without handing over the rest of the map.
She turned her face up to his.
“Help me!”
Curtis did not think.
His hands moved on their own. White fog uncoiled from his palms and wrapped them both — not a full vanish, not a spectacle for the street, just a smear of weather that made the corner forget two people had been standing in it. He took her hand and ran.
Braids flying. Blazer cutting the alley. The girl’s fingers locked in his like she had already decided he was the only solid thing left in Ikebukuro.
Curtis’s breath came sharp.
“What the hell am I doing?”
Behind them, the corner filled back in with ordinary traffic. Ahead, the workshop and the memorial box and the promise to Namie all collided into the same problem:
he had just grabbed the girl with the scar on her neck, hidden her in white fog, and started running —
and he still did not know whether he was rescuing her, collecting a sighting, or walking himself straight into the second half of a story Namie Yagiri had refused to name.
They reached somewhere safe.
An unused service corridor behind a row of shuttered shops — the kind of Ikebukuro leftover that did not ask questions if you arrived in a hurry. The white fog thinned and died against the concrete. Curtis put his back to the wall, long black braids settling, and finally looked at the girl properly. White cap. Pink tie. Scar on her neck. Breath coming too fast. She had not let go of his hand until the alley stopped moving.
His phone buzzed.
Celty:
curtis i need your help
shizuo and i were tracking a girl
we lost her
she has a scar on her neck
keep an eye out for me please curtis
Curtis stared at the screen.
Then he grimaced, the workshop flyer still folded in his pocket like a joke from a kinder hour.
“…Oh. Fuck all kinds of duck.”
The girl flinched at the sound of his voice. He locked the phone before the next message could land and tipped his head back against the wall, mismatched eyes on a ceiling that offered no protocol.
Namie wanted the girl with the scar.
Celty and Shizuo were hunting the girl with the scar.
The girl with the scar was currently three feet away from him, still wearing the word help on her face.
He had promised Namie he would keep an eye out.
He had just been asked by Celty to do the same.
Neither of them had said if you find her, hide her in fog and do not tell us.
Curtis dragged a hand down his face.
“Okay,” he muttered, not to the girl, not to the phone. “Okay. This is fine. This is extremely not fine.”
The memorial Gameboy would have to wait.
The dinner that was not a dinner would have to wait.
Justice, wherever he was laughing, would have to wait too.
Curtis Nightstar had run toward a workshop with a smile —
and ended up in an alley holding the one person in Ikebukuro that half the city wanted found.
Curtis’s thoughts moved faster than his mouth.
I can’t bring her to my home. If Celty comes by, this will get messy.
The real Celty. Helmet. Smoke. Last night. A text sitting on his phone asking him to hunt the girl he was currently hiding. Taking this one back to the furnished flat was how you turned a soft landing into a crime scene.
He turned to the girl.
White cap. Pink tie. Scar on her neck. Eyes still wide from the run.
“What’s your name?”
She swallowed, fingers tightening in the strap of her bag.
“My name is Celty.”
Curtis went very still.
The alley did not get quieter. It only felt like it did.
Oh. Fuuuuuck.
Not out loud. Not yet. The word lived behind his teeth while the rest of the morning stacked itself into a single impossible pile: Namie hunting a girl with a scar, Shizuo and his Celty tracking a girl with a scar, a girl in a white cap standing in front of him claiming the name of the headless woman who had woken up in his bed and asked him to keep an eye out.
Two Celtys.
One with a head.
One without.
And him in the middle with white fog on his hands and a Gameboy flyer still in his pocket.
Curtis dragged a breath in through his nose and did not let the panic reach his face all the way.
“…Celty,” he repeated, careful, like the name might bite. “Okay. We’re gonna stay right here a second. You’re safe. Nobody’s grabbing you while I’m standing here.”
He glanced at the locked phone, at the message from the other Celty, at the scar that had just become the most expensive detail in Ikebukuro.
I cannot take her home.
I cannot tell Namie.
I cannot tell Celty.
I cannot tell Shizuo.
Curtis Nightstar, who had wanted to build two Gameboys and put one in a memorial box, stood in a service corridor with a girl who had stolen a name he already loved —
and understood, with awful clarity, that the evening appointments had just become a war.
Curtis unlocked his phone and started hunting for a roof that was not his.
The first listing died immediately.
No. That’s too run-down.
The second died faster.
No. That one says it has bed bugs.
He scrolled, jaw tight, long black braids shifting as he angled the screen away from the girl who had just named herself Celty. A third option sat there like a bad compromise that would have to become a good one: central, clean, expensive enough that staff asked fewer of the wrong questions.
“…Hmm. Fine. This will do.”
He looked at her.
“Celty. Are you okay following me to a hotel? We need to get off the streets.”
She nodded. Small. Fast. The white cap dipped once, and that was enough.
The Uber arrived without ceremony. No fog this time — that had died in the alley the moment they stopped running. Just a tall foreign student in a blue blazer and a girl with a scar on her neck sitting in the back seat like two people late for something ordinary. Curtis kept his body between her and the window without making a speech about it. The driver did not ask.
The hotel was nicer than the day deserved.
Glass. Quiet carpet. The kind of lobby that smelled like money and citrus and did not care what name you wrote on the card as long as the card cleared. Curtis checked them in with the same even voice he had used on Namie, requested the fifth floor, and did not look like a boy who had thrown a man from a moving car before lunch.
The suite opened on too much space.
Two beds. High-roller quiet. A window on Ikebukuro that made the city look organized. Curtis let her in first, then shut the door and put the chain on like it meant something.
“You take that one,” he said, tipping his chin at the bed farther from the door. “I’ll take this one. Nobody comes in without me knowing. You hungry? Hurt? You can sit. You don’t have to explain the whole city to me in the first thirty seconds — but you are going to explain some of it.”
He set his bag down, the Gameboy flyer still folded inside it like a relic from a life that had wanted to solder plastic instead of hide a second Celty on the fifth floor.
Outside, Shizuo and the real Celty were still hunting a scar.
Across town, Namie was still waiting on a promise.
Inside the suite, Curtis Nightstar locked the door on a girl who wore a name that already belonged to someone else —
and tried to decide which lie would keep the most people alive until morning.
Curtis stayed by the door a second longer, then turned.
“What did you say your name is again?” Long black braids shifted against the blue blazer. Mismatched eyes held her without crowding the bed he had given her. “And could you tell me what you were running from?”
The girl sat on the edge of the far mattress, white cap still on, fingers worrying the pink strap of her bag. The scar on her neck showed every time she swallowed.
“Celty,” she said again. Same name. Same certainty, or the same script. “My name is Celty.”
A short breath.
“I was running from two people. A blond man — he was angry, he kept shouting, he threw things. And the Black Rider. The one on the motorcycle. They were chasing me. I don’t— I don’t know why they want me. I just know if they catch me I won’t get away again.”
Her voice had a mouth. That, more than the scar, sat wrong in Curtis’s chest. The Celty he knew did not speak into air like this. The Celty he knew arrived in a helmet and a private channel and a text that was still locked on his phone:
she has a scar on her neck
keep an eye out for me please curtis
He leaned his shoulder into the wall and did not let the grimace win.
“Shizuo and the Black Rider,” he said, translating it for himself more than for her. “Yeah. That tracks.”
The girl watched him like he might become a third hunter if she blinked.
Curtis held up one hand, easy.
“I’m not handing you to them tonight. You’re here. Door’s locked. Two beds. You stay on that one.” A pause. “Celty… I’m going to need you to sit tight while I figure out why half this city is looking for a girl with that scar. You don’t have to tell me your whole life. You do have to tell me if anyone else is coming. Yagiri. A boy named Seiji. Anyone who isn’t a blond bartender or a black motorcycle.”
He waited.
Outside, Ikebukuro kept hunting.
Inside the high-roller quiet, a girl who called herself Celty had just confirmed the exact chase the other Celty had asked him to join —
and Curtis Nightstar was now the only person in the room who knew both sides of the name.
Curtis turned toward the window and angled his phone so the girl on the far bed could not read the screen.
The real Celty’s message was still sitting unread beneath his thumb. He opened a different thread instead.
Curtis → Anri:
hey anri-chan
what was the name of your friend that went missing?
and i forgot — did you mention she was obsessed with seiji yagiri?
He hit send before he could dress it up into something that sounded less like an interrogation.
Behind him, the girl who called herself Celty sat on the high-roller bedspread with a scar on her neck and a white cap pulled low, watching his back like the street might come in through the glass. Two beds. Chain on the door. A workshop flyer still folded in his bag like a prayer from a dumber hour.
Curtis kept his face to the city.
If Anri said Mika, the pile got taller.
If Anri said yes, Seiji, the girl on the bed stopped being only a name collision and started being a Yagiri problem with a classmate’s missing-friend face.
His phone stayed quiet for one long breath.
Then it lit.
Curtis did not look at the girl when it did.
He only thought, very carefully, that he had promised Namie he would keep an eye out —
and that keeping an eye out was not supposed to mean hiding the answer in a suite Anri, Celty, and Shizuo did not know existed.
The phone lit in his hand.
Anri:
Mika.
Mika Harima.
Yes — she was… she is. Obsessed with Seiji-kun.
Curtis-kun, why? Did something happen? Did you see her?
Curtis closed his eyes for a second.
Mika.
Seiji.
A scar.
A name that was not hers.
He typed before the next question could pull him into a call he could not take with a girl sitting ten feet behind him.
Curtis:
thank you anri-chan
i know im asking a lot but i may need your help
its fine if you say no
He sent it and locked the screen, long black braids shifting as he glanced once toward the far bed. The girl who called herself Celty had not moved. White cap. Pink tie. The scar on her neck looking more expensive by the minute.
Behind the glass, Ikebukuro kept being a city that lost people.
On the other end of the thread, Anri Sonohara was already staring at his words with a missing friend’s name still warm in her hands, deciding whether help meant walking toward Mika —
or walking toward whatever Curtis Nightstar had found and could not put in a text.
Curtis went back to the girl.
He stayed on his side of the suite — door-bed, not hers — and let the question come out even.
“So. Do you know why the Black Rider and Shizuo were chasing you?” A small tip of his chin. “The angry bartender. He’s Shizuo.”
The girl pulled her knees up on the far mattress, white cap dipping as she watched him. The scar on her neck showed when she swallowed.
“They think I’m… something I’m not supposed to be.” Her voice stayed small, but it did not wobble into a lie so much as into a script she only half understood. “The blond one — Shizuo — he was shouting about a head. About a rider. The Black Rider didn’t talk. She just came. I ran. If I stop, they take me. That’s what I was told.”
A short breath.
“I didn’t steal anything. I didn’t hurt anyone. I was just with him, and then the city started chasing me like I belonged to someone else.”
Him.
Curtis filed the word without pouncing on it.
“With Seiji,” he said, not quite a question.
The girl’s fingers tightened on the pink strap.
She did not deny it.
Outside, the real Celty and Shizuo were still hunting a scar.
Inside the high-roller quiet, Mika-or-not-Mika-or-Celty-or-not-Celty sat on a bed she had not paid for and told a foreign student that the Black Rider wanted her for a head —
and Curtis Nightstar finally had enough of the shape to be afraid of how many people were telling the truth at once.
Curtis tipped his head, long black braids shifting against the blue blazer.
“Hmm. You and who?”
He left Seiji off the table on purpose this time. Let her put a name in the air if the name was real. Let the suite hear it from her mouth instead of his guess.
The girl looked at the carpet like the answer might cost her the locked door.
“…Seiji.” Quiet. Almost careful with it. “Seiji Yagiri. I was with him. Then people started coming. Then the bartender and the Black Rider. He told me to run if they found us. So I ran.”
She touched the scar on her neck without seeming to notice she had done it.
“He said they would take me away from him. That the rider wanted… this.” Her fingers stayed at the mark. “I don’t want to go back to a lab. I don’t want to go with the rider. I just want—”
She stopped.
Curtis waited. He did not finish the sentence for her.
Two beds. A chain on the door. Anri’s thread still warm in his pocket. Namie’s promise. Celty’s text. A girl on the fifth floor who had just handed him the exact name the morning car ride had been built around.
Seiji Yagiri.
Curtis Nightstar exhaled through his nose and wondered, not for the first time that day, how many people he could keep alive without telling any of them the whole truth.
Curtis did not move closer.
He looked at her face the way he had looked at Namie’s stain photos and Shinra’s desk — not hungry, not cruel. Inventory.
“Your face has been reconstructed to look like that.” Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes tracked the lines that did not belong to an ordinary accident. “And that scar on your neck is deliberate.”
The girl went rigid on the far bed.
Her hand flew to her cheek first, then down to the mark at her throat, as if she could hide the work if she covered it fast enough. The white cap dipped. For a second she looked younger than the suite, younger than the chase, younger than the name she had stolen.
“…Don’t say it like that.” The words came out thin. “Don’t— it’s me. This is my face. He said this is my face.”
A short, shaky breath.
“They told me it would help. That if I looked like this, he wouldn’t leave. That the scar had to be there or it wouldn’t be right.” Her fingers stayed on her neck. “I didn’t ask for the details. I asked if he would stay. They said yes if I became this.”
She swallowed.
“If you can see it, then other people can see it. That’s why they chase me. That’s why the rider wants me. That’s why I can’t go home.” Her eyes flicked to the door, then back to him. “Are you going to send me back to the people who did it?”
The high-roller quiet held the question.
Curtis Nightstar had just put a lab and a sister and a missing classmate on the same scar —
and the girl who called herself Celty was waiting to find out whether the boy who hid her in fog was rescue, or the next pair of hands in a reconstruction she had already survived once.
Curtis kept his voice even.
“You’re Mika. The girl that’s missing.”
The name hit the suite harder than Shizuo’s chase had.
She stared at him like he had opened a door she had been told no longer existed. The white cap, the reconstructed face, the deliberate scar — all of it tried to stay Celty for one more second and failed in her eyes first.
“…Don’t.” Thin. Immediate. “Don’t call me that. That’s not— Mika’s gone. Mika was before. He doesn’t want Mika. He wants—”
Her hand tightened on the scar.
“He said if I kept that name I would lose him. So I’m Celty. I’m Celty now.” A short, broken breath. “How do you even know Mika? Nobody who helps me is supposed to know that name.”
She looked at the locked door, then at the foreign student who had hidden her in fog and paid for two beds instead of a lab.
“If you know I’m Mika,” she whispered, “then you know Anri. And if you know Anri, then the whole city is closer than I thought.”
Curtis did not confirm Anri.
He did not have to.
The missing girl was sitting on a high-roller bedspread wearing someone else’s name, and the only thing left in her face that still belonged to Mika Harima was the fear that the old name might send her back to the people who had carved the new one on.
Curtis stayed where he was — close enough to be a wall, not close enough to be another pair of hands on her face.
“You’re safe with me.” The words came out quieter than the alley. Long black braids shifted as he tipped his chin toward the chained door. “Please calm down. It would take highly analytical people to notice.”
Mika — Celty — the girl on the far bed held still, fingers still at the scar like she could keep the old name from leaking out through it.
For a second she only breathed.
Then some of the panic loosened, not because the city had gotten kinder, but because the boy who had seen the reconstruction had not reached for a phone or a lab or a blond bartender.
“…Analytical,” she repeated, almost a laugh that failed. “You noticed.”
“I look at things too long,” Curtis said. “Most people don’t. Street crowd doesn’t. A hunter looking for a scar is different from a stranger looking at a face. You’re not walking around with a sign on you. You’re walking around with work that was meant to fool someone specific.”
She swallowed.
“Seiji.”
He did not deny it.
Mika’s shoulders dropped a fraction. The white cap stayed on. When she spoke again, the voice was smaller and less scripted.
“…You’re not going to call the rider.” Not quite a question. “And you’re not going to call the people who did this. You said I’m safe. People say that. Then they trade you.”
Her eyes flicked to his mismatched ones, searching for the hook.
“If you’re lying, tell me now. I can still run. If you’re not…” A thinner breath. “Then I need a minute to stop shaking before I try to be a person again.”
Curtis nodded once.
“Take the minute. Door stays locked. I’m not trading you.”
He did not add the rest — that half the city was already looking, that safe had a time limit, that Anri’s name was sitting in his pocket like a second key.
On the far bed, Mika Harima tried to believe a foreign student with analytical eyes and a soft voice.
For the first time since the corner, her hands left her neck.
Curtis let the quiet sit until her hands had left her neck.
Then he asked the only question that did not belong to Namie, or Anri, or the rider on the other end of his phone.
“What would you like me to call you?”
The girl stared at him.
Not at the lock. Not at the scar. At the fact that the boy who had already seen through the work was offering her the name instead of assigning one.
“…Celty.” It came out automatic, then faltered. She touched the cap brim like it might hold the script in place. “He calls me that. If you call me Mika, it feels like you’re sending me back.”
A short breath.
“But you already know. So if you say Mika when we’re alone… I can live with it. Just not in front of him. Not if he finds us.” Her eyes flicked to the second bed, then back. “Don’t call me both. Pick one when you talk to me. I can’t be two people in the same room. I already did that once and it broke.”
Curtis nodded once.
“Alright. In here, I’ll use what you answer to. Out there, I won’t put a name on you that gets you grabbed.”
He did not tell her which name he had chosen behind his teeth.
Mika. Celty. The missing girl. The reconstructed face.
On the fifth floor of a hotel she had not paid for, she waited to hear which one the foreign student would actually use —
and Curtis Nightstar understood that whatever he called her next would decide whether she stayed on the bed, or ran for the street again.
Curtis stayed on his side of the suite, voice even.
“So why not tell me about yourself. You can drop the amnesia act.” Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers without crowding. “I’m not going to judge you. All I heard were rumors about you. I don’t like to listen to rumors. I like to get to know the person. Most of the time rumors embellish and leave out things.”
The girl sat with that longer than the chase had given her room to sit with anything.
The cap brim dipped. For a second the Celty script tried to climb back into her mouth — I don’t remember, I was just running, I don’t know why they want me — and then it died on the fact that he had already said Mika and reconstructed and deliberate.
“…The rumors are probably that I was a stalker.” Quiet. Not quite ashamed, not quite defiant. “That I followed Seiji. That I used Anri. That I disappeared because I was crazy.”
A short breath.
“Some of that’s true. I did follow him. After he stepped in one time I couldn’t stop. Anri was my friend and I still made her into a way to stay close to him. I’m not going to pretty that up so you’ll like me better.” Her fingers worried the pink strap. “Then I saw something I wasn’t supposed to see, and the world turned into labs and promises. They said if I became this face, he would stay. So I became this face. I didn’t forget who I was. I just… stopped being allowed to be her if I wanted him to look at me.”
She glanced at Curtis, searching for the flinch.
“That’s the part rumors leave out. Not the obsession. Everybody likes that part. They leave out that I said yes. That I sat there and let them change me because the alternative was him walking away from Mika and never coming back.” A thinner beat. “If you’re waiting for me to be only a victim or only a monster, I can’t do that. I’m both. That’s why I ran when the rider and the bartender came. I don’t know which version they want to take — the girl I was, or the girl I paid for.”
The high-roller quiet held her.
Curtis Nightstar had asked for the person under the rumors.
Mika Harima had given him a yes that cost a face —
and she watched his mismatched eyes to see whether knowing the person was still something he wanted.
Curtis’s voice dropped the same way it had in Namie’s car.
“How sad.” No smile. No inventory edge. Long black braids shifted as he looked at her and did not look away from the reconstruction. “I’m sorry you went through all that, Mika.”
The name and the apology hit together.
She froze on the far bed, white cap brim trembling once before she stilled it. For a second the old reflex tried to stand up — don’t call me that, I’m Celty, he doesn’t want Mika — and then it ran into a foreign student who had just said sorry like he had said it to a Yagiri sister with blood on his face and nothing to collect.
“…You keep doing that.” Thin. Almost accusing. “You see the worst thing and then you say you’re sorry like it costs you something.”
A short breath.
“Don’t be sorry for me. I chose it. I sat there. I said yes so he would stay.” Her fingers found the scar and left it again. “If you’re sorry, be sorry I still want him after that. That’s the part that makes people look at me like I’m broken.”
She swallowed.
“…But thank you. For saying my name like I’m still a person in it. Most people who figure it out only want to know where he is, or who cut me, or whether I’m useful.” Her eyes flicked to his. “You’re looking at me like the rumor isn’t the whole file. I don’t know what to do with that either.”
A thinner beat, quieter.
“If you keep calling me Mika in here… I’ll answer. Just don’t say it like you’re taking Seiji away from me. I can survive the truth. I can’t survive being turned back into a missing poster while I’m still sitting here.”
Curtis nodded once.
On the fifth floor, two different women in one day had been told I’m sorry by the same boy —
and Mika Harima, who had bought a face to be loved, sat with the strange, dangerous feeling that someone had seen the receipt and still offered her a name that belonged to her.
Curtis stayed on his side of the suite and let the words come out slow, the same way he had talked a girl down from a roof and a class rep down from a hallway.
“You’re safe right now. No one here wants to hurt you. You don’t have to hide or pretend to be someone else.”
Mika’s mouth tightened. He kept going.
“You keep talking about Seiji like he’s the only thing that matters… but you matter too. You’re allowed to want more than just being someone he looks at.”
A short breath.
“What happened to you was terrifying. Anyone would be scared. But you don’t have to face it alone. You don’t have to chase someone who won’t protect you.”
Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers.
“You’re Mika Harima. Not Celty. Not a replacement. You’re someone who deserves to be seen for who you are.”
He finished quieter.
“If you’re scared, you can talk to me. I won’t judge you. I just want you to be okay.”
The high-roller room took the speech and made it occupy both beds.
Mika stared at him like he had reached into the reconstruction and tried to pull a girl out of it by hand. For a long second she did not breathe right. Then her face crumpled in a small, furious way — not at him, at the fact that the words fit a wound she had been calling love.
“…Stop.” It came out wrecked, not loud. “You can’t just say I’m allowed to want more. I already spent the more. I spent my face. I spent Anri. I spent the version of me that could still go home.”
A shaky inhale.
“He looks at this.” She touched the scar, then her cheek. “If I go back to Mika, he looks through me. That’s not a rumor. That’s what happened before they cut. You’re telling me I deserve to be seen and I believe you mean it. I just don’t know if I want to be seen if being seen means he walks away.”
She wiped at her eye with the heel of her hand, angry at the wetness.
“…I’ll talk to you. I can do that. I can sit here and not run for ten minutes. Don’t ask me to stop wanting him tonight. I can be Mika in this room. I can’t be free of him in the same hour somebody finally uses my real name like it isn’t a curse.”
A thinner beat.
“Thank you. For not making me Celty just because that’s the face they built. That’s… more than Seiji ever did.”
Curtis let her keep Seiji for the night.
He had put Mika Harima back in the file.
Whether she could live in it was a different appointment —
one the locked door would have to hold until morning.
Curtis did not let the silence turn her back into a missing poster.
“You’re not obsessed.” The correction was quiet, not scolding. Long black braids shifted as he held her eyes. “You’re hurt. You’re trying to hold onto the first person who made you feel special. But you don’t have to cling to that pain anymore.”
Mika’s fingers stilled on the pink strap.
“You don’t have to chase someone who runs from you. You can choose people who stay.”
A short breath.
“You don’t have to be Celty to be worth something. You don’t have to be anyone except yourself.”
The suite went very still.
She looked at him like the words were a door she had been told did not exist, and now someone was holding it open with no lab on the other side. For a second the reconstruction, the cap, the scar, the name Seiji had given the face — all of it tried to argue. Then her mouth trembled and the argument lost.
“…People who stay,” she whispered. “I don’t know what that looks like. Anri stayed and I still used her. Seiji stayed for a neck and a face that isn’t mine. You hid me and paid for two beds and you keep talking like I’m allowed to get off the floor.”
A wet, angry little laugh.
“If I stop chasing him, what’s left? A missing girl with someone else’s cheekbones and a friend I haven’t earned back. You’re making it sound simple. It isn’t simple. It’s the only story I have.”
She wiped her face again, then nodded once, small, as if agreeing to a truce instead of a conversion.
“I’ll try to hear you. That’s all I can do tonight. I can sit here as Mika. I can stop saying Celty at you like a shield. Don’t ask me to choose people who stay before I even know if I’m one of them.”
Her eyes found his mismatched ones.
“You stayed this long without trading me. That’s… new. Don’t disappear while I’m still deciding if I believe the rest.”
Curtis tipped his chin toward the door.
“I’m not disappearing. Door stays locked. You stay Mika in here. That’s enough for now.”
On the fifth floor, the chase paused.
Not ended.
Paused —
long enough for a reconstructed girl to try on her own name without a boy named Seiji in the room to take it away.